openstack-glance — OpenStack Image service (Glance) 33.0.0 (2026.2 Hibiscus)
=============================================================================

This package installs the Glance image service. It installs software
ONLY: nothing is enabled, started, migrated or credentialed at install
time.

What it ships
-------------

  /usr/bin/glance-*                 administrative CLIs (api, manage,
                                    status, scrubber, cache tools, ...)
  glance.wsgi.api:application       the supported WSGI application
  glance/db/sqlalchemy/alembic_migrations/   database migration scripts
  /etc/glance/glance-api-paste.ini  upstream paste pipeline (wiring)
  /etc/glance/*.conf.sample         generated configuration samples
  /etc/glance/schema-image.json     image schema (read-only data)
  /etc/init.d/glance                OpenRC service (gunicorn)
  /usr/lib/systemd/system/glance.service     systemd service (gunicorn)
  /etc/conf.d/glance                WSGI container runtime settings

Operator steps (NOT performed by this package)
----------------------------------------------

1. Write the configuration from the samples:

     cp /etc/glance/glance-api.conf.sample /etc/glance/glance-api.conf
     # edit /etc/glance/glance-api.conf (database, keystone auth,
     # stores, ...). No database driver is installed by this package:
     # add python-pymysql for MariaDB, or use sqlite for trials.

2. Initialise the database:

     glance-manage db_sync

3. Register the service and endpoints in Keystone (operator-chosen):

     openstack service create ... (see the deployment plan)

4. Enable and start the service explicitly:

     rc-update add glance default && rc-service glance start
     # or
     systemctl enable --now glance

Glance is served by gunicorn; nginx/haproxy fronting it for TLS is an
optional, operator-configured choice.

Independence
------------

Glance depends on the native python closure, gunicorn and the keystone
client libraries. It does not depend on Nova, Neutron, Cinder or QEMU,
and it can be installed on its own.
